  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nScattered showers lingered across the area this afternoon as a \nshortwave trough evident on water vapor imagery was lifting slowly \nnortheast across the area. As this feature continues to drift north \nand east away from the area tonight, large-scale forcing for \nsubsidence will allow shower activity to taper off as we move \nthrough the evening hours. With a lack of scouring of the low level \nmoisture, low clouds and fog may expand across the area after \nmidnight with some patchy dense fog possible at times. The low \nclouds and fog could linger into the morning hours on Sunday and \nwith limited insolation, afternoon highs may struggle to climb out \nof the 60s for most areas. Forecast highs on Sunday were trended \nlower while remaining mild for early Nov.\n\nShortwave ridging will build over the Central Plains on Sunday ahead \nof a vigorous mid/upper trough building into the Central Great Basin \narea. Tranquil weather conditions are anticipated with breezy \nsoutherly winds developing as we move through the day. Breezy \nsoutherly winds on Sunday night will result in mild overnight \nconditions with lows around 60 for most areas. A record warm low \ntemperature is possible at KRSL with a forecast low of 59.\n\nAs the LLJ ramps up late Sunday night after 05-07Z, we could see \nsome elevated storms developing along the nose of the LLJ. Some \nstrong or marginally severe storms may be possible given steep mid-\nlvl lapse rates and some elevated buoyancy but some residual capping \naround H75 may limit more robust activity. In the absence of more \nrobust early morning activity, we could see more widespread \nthunderstorm activity as we move into the early to mid-afternoon \nhours along a developing dryline in parts of south central Kansas. \nRich moisture in the pbl along with considerable wind shear weighted \nin the lowest 2 km could allow for some storm organization, \nespecially early in the initiation process leading to some supercell \nstructures before we transition to more of a linear mode given the \norientation of the deep layer shear vectors with respect to the \nmoist sector. Damaging winds may be the largest threat although some \nmarginally severe hail and a tornado or two will be hard to rule \nout. The severe weather threat should wane by 04-06Z at the \nlatest while some post frontal showers with embedded storms \ncould linger into the Tuesday before the activity gradually \ndiminishes from west to east as the mid/upper trough translates \neastward on Tue. The GFS continues to be the most progressive \nwith this feature.\n\nMild, above average temperatures are anticipated in the wake of this \nsystem as we head into the latter portion of the week and the \nupcoming weekend. Depending on how quickly this system departs, we \ncould see a couple of other shortwave troughs rotate over the \nCentral Plains bringing low probabilities for showers late in the \nweek and into the upcoming weekend but confidence remains rather low \nat this time.",
